  "id": "Compound::DB01076 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Atorvastatin, a widely used statin, is implicated in the pathogenesis of hypertension through its interaction with Ugt1a3 and Ugt2b7 enzymes, which are involved in the metabolism of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and other antihypertensive agents. The evidence suggests that Atorvastatin may modulate the pharmacokinetics of these enzymes, potentially affecting the clearance of drugs like Losartan and Irbesartan, which are commonly used to treat hypertension. This mechanism could lead to altered drug exposure and, in turn, influence the efficacy or safety of these therapies. The shared pathway involving Ugt1a3 and Ugt2b7, with a common ancestor at Organic Anion, supports a unified mechanism of action that links Atorvastatin to the treatment of hypertension. The observed scores indicate a plausible, though not definitive, relationship, which would require further experimental validation to confirm the clinical relevance.",
